Q: WebAssembly software demo - Read from file A: <p>If you’re using Qt with WebAssembly, <code>QFileDialog::getOpenFileContent()</code> is probably the easiest way to handle this.</p>
<p>It lets the user choose a local file and gives you the file contents directly as a <code>QByteArray</code>:</p>
<pre><code>QFileDialog::getOpenFileContent(
    &quot;Excel files (*.xlsx)&quot;,
    [](const QString &amp;fileName, const QByteArray &amp;data)
    {
        if (fileName.isEmpty())
            return;

        qDebug() &lt;&lt; fileName;
        qDebug() &lt;&lt; &quot;File size:&quot; &lt;&lt; data.size();

        // Process data here
    }
);
</code></pre>
<p>This works well for a WASM app because the browser doesn't allow direct access to the user's filesystem. The user selects the file, and Qt gives your application the contents.</p>
<p>For a simple file-reading demo, I'd use this approach rather than adding extra code around file handling.</p>

Q: External link Alternative for JS A: <p><strong>EDIT:</strong>
For hosting the JS source file on some external server for testing you can use sites like <a href="http://yourjavascript.com" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://yourjavascript.com</a>
You can search for more alternatives on Google but this one worked well for me :)</p>

<p><strong>Original Answer:</strong>
As others pointed out in comments, you can simply put the source url of JavaScript program in src attribute of <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag...</p>

<pre><code>&lt;html&gt;
    &lt;head&gt;
        &lt;script src="https://usscript1.000webhostapp.com/example1.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
    &lt;/head&gt;
    &lt;body&gt;...your website's body here...&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>Regarding the blocking issue, make sure your src link to the JavaScript file is correct (by opening the link in seperate browser tab or have look at the console in inspect page window for any errors).</p>

<p>You can put the script on the same site and link to it as,</p>

<pre><code>&lt;html&gt;
    &lt;head&gt;
        &lt;script src="/example1.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
    &lt;/head&gt;
    &lt;body&gt;...your website's body here...&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>If you are facing issues with putting the file on your site, you can directly put the code of JavaScript program inside <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag like this...</p>

<pre><code>&lt;html&gt;
    &lt;head&gt;
        &lt;script&gt;
            ...your JS program goes here...
        &lt;/script&gt;
    &lt;/head&gt;
    &lt;body&gt;...your website's body here...&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: How to make a button that switches between two colours? A: <p>A more optimised solution</p>

<pre><code>var login = document.querySelector('#login');
var signup = document.querySelector('#signup');

function changeColor(elem1, elem2) {
  elem1.style.backgroundColor = "#a8661f";
  elem2.style.backgroundColor = "#d18029";
}
login.onclick = function() {
  changeColor(login, signup);
}
signup.onclick = function() {
  changeColor(signup, login);
}
</code></pre>

<p>I hope it helps.</p>
